<article class="env-remark" id="09W7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09W7">Remark <span data-tag="09W7">7.20.5</span>.</a> Let $u : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}$ be a functor between categories. Given morphisms $g : u(U) \to V$ and $f : W \to V$ in $\mathcal{D}$ we can consider the functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{C}^{opp} \longrightarrow \textit{Sets},\quad T \longmapsto \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {C}(T, U) \times _{\mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {D}(u(T), V)} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}\nolimits _\mathcal {D}(u(T), W)  \]
</div>
  <p> If this functor is representable, denote $U \times _{g, V, f} W$ the corresponding object of $\mathcal{C}$. Assume that $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ are sites. Consider the property $P$: for every covering $\{ f_ j : V_ j \to V\} $ of $\mathcal{D}$ and any morphism $g : u(U) \to V$ we have </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$U \times _{g, V, f_ i} V_ i$ exists for all $i$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\{ U \times _{g, V, f_ i} V_ i \to U\} $ is a covering of $\mathcal{C}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Please note the similarity with the definition of continuous functors. If $u$ has $P$ then $u$ is cocontinuous (details omitted). Many of the cocontinuous functors we will encounter satisfy $P$. </p>
</article>